# DTF tier manifest: native-client
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + the three real client containers
# (client.dnf = Fedora, client.apt = Debian, client.docker = dind + a local
# upstream OCI registry). The tier drives the REAL advertised client route:
#   * RPM (#2580): publish an RPM, then a real `dnf install` in the Fedora
#     client that RESOLVES AND FOLLOWS the repodata `<location href>` and
#     asserts the package actually installs (not just that repodata lists it).
#   * deb   (#2580 analogue): the apt equivalent — real `apt-get install`
#     following the Packages `Filename:` field.
#   * docker (#2477): a real `docker pull` through an AK Docker REMOTE (proxy)
#     repo that forces >=2 OCI token exchanges re-presenting the same offline
#     token; the pull must SUCCEED (no family revocation).
#
# NOTE (deviation from design brick-3 note "ALSO pull in proxy=squid"): the
# squid overlay is purpose-built for #2570 (a mock nginx origin on a 172.16/12
# net + an SSRF CIDR allowlist + HTTP_PROXY forcing all backend egress through
# squid). It is NOT a Docker registry and its HTTP_PROXY/CIDR wiring would stop
# the backend reaching an OCI upstream, breaking the pull. #2477's discriminator
# is the >=2 token-exchange offline-token REUSE on the client<->AK path, which
# is independent of egress-proxy topology. This tier therefore stands up a
# self-contained local `upstream-registry` (registry:2) as the proxy source
# instead, keeping the leg deterministic and offline. See oracle.sh header.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + repo create + an
# RPM/deb publish + repeated OCI token exchanges back-to-back through a single
# admin user; the base default (ON) would throttle them. The base default stays
# ON; a tier that must disable a shipped control does so explicitly (design 2.2,
# same as the migration and proxy-egress tiers).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem client.dnf client.apt client.docker"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
